{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794571504440,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a18a6ffeJYEGLs0dTSiOZ0","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4cc07d1001tb0hY6XcGoq0tq","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a18a6ffeJYEGLs0dTSiOZ0","messageID":"msg_1d4cbfb44001Yd3z82w08f5Siz","type":"tool","id":"call_f21bb467574544a1b84b81c0","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":40,"offset":55,"path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md"},"output":"Read file specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md, lines 55-94\n55: | **Handoff Criteria** | Each phase meets its exit check in the Phase Handoff Criteria table before the next phase starts |\n56: <!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n57: \n58: ---\n59: \n60: <!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n61: ## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n62: \n63: ### Problem Statement\n64: The repository ships 44 tracked slash commands that own complete workflows, but the skill advisor reaches the model with skills. Only `/doctor:*` commands arrive as a runnable line, 22 of 62 command bridge rows are off in the TypeScript scorer, the recommendation carries no command field and no rule tells a model what to do with a surfaced command. A user who chats without typing a command therefore gets an improvised approach where a command workflow exists. The evidence is in `../007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/research.md` and the independent review `../007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/synthesis-review.md`.\n65: \n66: ### Purpose\n67: Deliver the review's recommendation set (R1 to R10, L1 to L12, G1 to G7) in the order its build plan gives, under the operator decisions D1 to D5 recorded in section 13 of `../007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/research.md`. When the last phase closes, a plain chat request surfaces the matching command beside its skill with a run, start or ask policy, the model picks between the command path and the skill path, and a measured baseline shows what changed.\n68: \n69: > **Phase-parent note:** This spec.md is the ONLY authored document at the parent level. All detailed planning, task breakdowns, checklists, and decisions live in the child phase folders listed in the Phase Documentation Map below. This keeps the parent from drifting stale as phases execute and pivot.\n70: <!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n71: \n72: ---\n73: \n74: <!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n75: ## 3. SCOPE\n76: \n77: ### In Scope\n78: - Measure command routing before changing it: a command prompt corpus and a baseline for the 44 tracked commands.\n79: - Generate one command inventory with a risk class and a derived policy per command and per doctor route.\n80: - Make plain chat reach a command: attach the command to its owner skill for hub commands, and author intent phrases for commands with no hub mode.\n81: - Reach a command from a paraphrase that shares no words with it, by matching the prompt's meaning against a vector per command.\n82: - Extend the recommendation and the brief with the command, its policy and its owner skill.\n83: - Bind the behaviour in `AGENTS.md` Gate 2 and in a generated pointer for runtimes with no brief.\n84: - Prove the behaviour per runtime from transcripts.\n85: \n86: ### Out of Scope\n87: - Changing how skills are scored against each other. The four existing accuracy numbers are a floor in every phase.\n88: - Surfacing the private, untracked `/local-skilled-alignment` command (decision D5).\n89: - Forcing the command path. Decision D2 keeps the model free to pick the skill path.\n90: - Adding a command front door to a skill that has none.\n91: \n92: ### Files to Change\n93: Audit trail only. Each child's `plan.md` holds the detail, and phases 5 to 7 are not planned yet, so their rows name the area the review points at.\n94: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 95]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md, lines 55-94\n55: | **Handoff Criteria** | Each phase meets its exit check in the Phase Handoff Criteria table before the next phase starts |\n56: <!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n57: \n58: ---\n59: \n60: <!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n61: ## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n62: \n63: ### Problem Statement\n64: The repository ships 44 tracked slash commands that own complete workflows, but the skill advisor reaches the model with skills. Only `/doctor:*` commands arrive as a runnable line, 22 of 62 command bridge rows are off in the TypeScript scorer, the recommendation carries no command field and no rule tells a model what to do with a surfaced command. A user who chats without typing a command therefore gets an improvised approach where a command workflow exists. The evidence is in `../007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/research.md` and the independent review `../007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/synthesis-review.md`.\n65: \n66: ### Purpose\n67: Deliver the review's recommendation set (R1 to R10, L1 to L12, G1 to G7) in the order its build plan gives, under the operator decisions D1 to D5 recorded in section 13 of `../007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/research.md`. When the last phase closes, a plain chat request surfaces the matching command beside its skill with a run, start or ask policy, the model picks between the command path and the skill path, and a measured baseline shows what changed.\n68: \n69: > **Phase-parent note:** This spec.md is the ONLY authored document at the parent level. All detailed planning, task breakdowns, checklists, and decisions live in the child phase folders listed in the Phase Documentation Map below. This keeps the parent from drifting stale as phases execute and pivot.\n70: <!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n71: \n72: ---\n73: \n74: <!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n75: ## 3. SCOPE\n76: \n77: ### In Scope\n78: - Measure command routing before changing it: a command prompt corpus and a baseline for the 44 tracked commands.\n79: - Generate one command inventory with a risk class and a derived policy per command and per doctor route.\n80: - Make plain chat reach a command: attach the command to its owner skill for hub commands, and author intent phrases for commands with no hub mode.\n81: - Reach a command from a paraphrase that shares no words with it, by matching the prompt's meaning against a vector per command.\n82: - Extend the recommendation and the brief with the command, its policy and its owner skill.\n83: - Bind the behaviour in `AGENTS.md` Gate 2 and in a generated pointer for runtimes with no brief.\n84: - Prove the behaviour per runtime from transcripts.\n85: \n86: ### Out of Scope\n87: - Changing how skills are scored against each other. The four existing accuracy numbers are a floor in every phase.\n88: - Surfacing the private, untracked `/local-skilled-alignment` command (decision D5).\n89: - Forcing the command path. Decision D2 keeps the model free to pick the skill path.\n90: - Adding a command front door to a skill that has none.\n91: \n92: ### Files to Change\n93: Audit trail only. Each child's `plan.md` holds the detail, and phases 5 to 7 are not planned yet, so their rows name the area the review points at.\n94: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 95]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\":40,\"offset\":55,\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794571503569,"end":1794571504440}}}}
